folder for local configurations that do not get overwritten with updates

pre-*.tcl will be sourced early in dsconf.tcl (before ess is initialized)

post-*.tcl will be sourced at end of dsconf.tcl (after ess is initialized)

<name>.tcl (camera, extio, input, juicer, mesh, openephys, slider,
sound) is sourced by that subprocess's own config, near the end of it.

rig.tcl is different: it is the settings module's file -- `setting <sub>
<key> <value>` lines written by `settings::put -persist` and edited from
the settings gear rather than by hand. It is parsed per-statement in a
safe interp, so one bad line costs that one line (with a breadcrumb in
settings/parse_errors/*), never the boot.

A file here named <something>.retired-<date> was renamed at boot by
config/rig_settings.tcl because NOTHING sources that name any more (its
subsystem was retired, or the name never had a consumer). The content is
untouched -- the suffix is the whole message. Delete them when convenient;
re-creating the original name just gets it retired again at the next boot.

offline (empty marker file) puts dserv in offline / air-gapped mode:
all outbound network to non-loopback hosts is refused instantly and the
registry/mesh/trial-ingest paths skip cleanly. Equivalent to exporting
DSERV_OFFLINE=1. See docs/OFFLINE.md.


WHAT AN ERROR IN ONE OF THESE FILES COSTS

Apart from input's two files (input.tcl and the machine-written
input_devices.tcl, both catch-guarded), none of these are sourced inside a
catch, and every one of them is hand-written per rig, which makes them the
likeliest source of a boot that does not finish. What you lose depends on
which file threw:

  pre-*.tcl    aborts dsconf.tcl at the very top. NO subprocesses start at
               all. This is the most expensive place to make a mistake.

  post-*.tcl   aborts config/essconf.tcl. dserv keeps booting (the rest of
               dsconf.tcl still runs) but `ess` is left half-initialized --
               typically no system loaded, and ::ess::init refuses to run.
               post-pins.tcl is the usual suspect here: a gpioLineRequest
               for a line that is missing, already claimed, or not yet
               released by a previous dserv throws like anything else.

  <name>.tcl   aborts that subprocess's config only. The subprocess stays
               alive and reachable, but half-configured.

In every case the failure is recorded rather than silent: <name>/init_error
carries the message and the full errorInfo goes to the systemd journal.
Nothing prints on the console, so you have to look.


CHECKING THAT A BOOT ACTUALLY FINISHED

  # did dsconf.tcl reach its last line?      1 = yes
  dservGet system/boot_complete

  # if not, the last step it attempted
  dservGet system/boot_stage

  # any subprocess whose config threw
  dservKeys *init_error*
  dservGet ess/init_error          ;# etc, for whatever the line above lists

From a shell:

  printf 'dservKeys *init_error*\n' | nc -q1 localhost 2570

If dserv does not answer at all, a config script is BLOCKED rather than
broken -- config scripts run synchronously, so one that waits on hardware
(serial, USB, ALSA, a stim2 connect) hangs the whole boot, and the control
port then accepts connections but never replies. The journal is the only
channel left: look for the last

  dsconf: starting <name>

with no matching "started <name> ok". That names the subprocess that hung.

scripts/boot_soak.sh restarts dserv repeatedly and captures all of the
above on the first bad boot, for chasing intermittent ones.


WRITING THESE FILES

Datapoints owned by another subprocess may not exist yet, or at all if that
subprocess failed. A bare dservGet of an absent datapoint RAISES, so guard
any cross-subprocess read:

  if { [dservExists ess/system] } { set sys [dservGet ess/system] }

`after ms script` does NOT work here -- there is no Tcl event loop. Use
dservWhen, dservAfter, or the timer module instead.
